      },
      {
        "id": "E2",
        "kind": "body",
        "text": "Multi-vector embedding models give each query token its own opportunity to find a match in a document.",
        "sourceUrl": "https://huggingface.co/blog/token-pooling",
        "source": "Hugging Face Blog",
        "publishedAt": "2026-11-11T00:00:00.000Z",
        "checkedAt": "2026-11-13T17:42:50.580Z"
      },
      {
        "id": "E3",
        "kind": "body",
        "text": "Token pooling-aware training raises that retention to 97.8% at the same storage budget!",
        "sourceUrl": "https://huggingface.co/blog/token-pooling",
        "source": "Hugging Face Blog",
        "publishedAt": "2026-11-11T00:00:00.000Z",
        "checkedAt": "2026-11-13T17:42:50.580Z"
      },
      {
        "id": "E4",
        "kind": "body",
        "text": "This post builds on pooling-aware training studied in Stefan Josef's Learn to Pool and LightOn's pooling regularization study , which also explores targeted training and adaptive budgets.",
        "sourceUrl": "https://huggingface.co/blog/token-pooling",
        "source": "Hugging Face Blog",
        "publishedAt": "2026-11-11T00:00:00.000Z",
        "checkedAt": "2026-11-13T17:42:50.580Z"
      },
      {
        "id": "E5",
        "kind": "body",
        "text": "Multi-vector models such as ColBERT instead keep a collection of contextual token vectors, allowing different parts of a query to match different parts of a document.",
        "sourceUrl": "https://huggingface.co/blog/token-pooling",
        "source": "Hugging Face Blog",
        "publishedAt": "2026-11-11T00:00:00.000Z",
        "checkedAt": "2026-11-13T17:42:50.580Z"
      },
      {
        "id": "E6",
        "kind": "body",
        "text": "Pooling-aware training applies the objective to representations with fewer token vectors.",
        "sourceUrl": "https://huggingface.co/blog/token-pooling",
        "source": "Hugging Face Blog",
        "publishedAt": "2026-11-11T00:00:00.000Z",
        "checkedAt": "2026-11-13T17:42:50.580Z"
      },
      {
        "id": "E7",
        "kind": "body",
        "text": "My training script uses mini-batches of 64 with a training batch size of 256.",
        "sourceUrl": "https://huggingface.co/blog/token-pooling",
        "source": "Hugging Face Blog",
        "publishedAt": "2026-11-11T00:00:00.000Z",
        "checkedAt": "2026-11-13T17:42:50.580Z"
      },
      {
        "id": "E8",
        "kind": "body",
        "text": "uv run examples/multi_vector_encoder/training/token_pooling/training_cached_contrastive.py --baseline",
        "sourceUrl": "https://huggingface.co/blog/token-pooling",
        "source": "Hugging Face Blog",
        "publishedAt": "2026-11-11T00:00:00.000Z",
        "checkedAt": "2026-11-13T17:42:50.580Z"
      },
      {
        "id": "E9",
        "kind": "body",
        "text": "To investigate, I trained two models from answerdotai/ModernBERT-base , each with a fresh 128-dimensional token projection:",
        "sourceUrl": "https://huggingface.co/blog/token-pooling",
        "source": "Hugging Face Blog",
        "publishedAt": "2026-11-11T00:00:00.000Z",
        "checkedAt": "2026-11-13T17:42:50.580Z"
      },
      {
        "id": "E10",
        "kind": "body",
        "text": "The training token cap is 300, and short queries are expanded to at least 32 token positions to provide additional contextual query vectors.",
        "sourceUrl": "https://huggingface.co/blog/token-pooling",
        "source": "Hugging Face Blog",
        "publishedAt": "2026-11-11T00:00:00.000Z",
